# Standalone first, website second

Any time a standalone tool changes — the accessibility checker, the search engine ranker, or a future tool that also appears on the site — update that tool’s own GitHub repo first. Only then copy the same product change onto the website.

## Source of truth

| Tool | Edit first (standalone) | Copy second (website) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Accessibility checker | https://github.com/jorgepaezdev/accessibility-checker | `/accessibility-checker` on https://github.com/jorgepaezdev/website |
| Search ranker | https://github.com/jorgepaezdev/search-engine-tracker | `/search-engine-ranking-tool` on https://github.com/jorgepaezdev/website |

The standalone repo is canonical. The website page is a downstream copy of the same product.

This repo is the source of truth for the search ranker. After a change here is committed and pushed, copy it onto the website `/search-engine-ranking-tool` page.

## Required order

1. Implement, test, commit, and push in the standalone GitHub repo.
2. Then port that same product change onto the matching website page: behavior, copy, empty/error/loading states.
3. Never start a tool change on the website. If a request only mentions the site page, still update the standalone repo first, then copy it over.
4. Do not ship a website-only tool change and sync later.

## What “copy” means

Match the user-facing product. Stacks may differ (Python standalone vs Next.js site) unless Jorge asks to embed the standalone app. Port the product change; do not dump the Python app into Next.js by default.

## Git

Sync exclusively with GitHub. Never Origin.
